1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,525
The Future of Space Travel.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:11,787
Human spaceflight was exclusively government-funded for the first four decades of the space age, with the US and Soviet space programs spending billions per launch.

3
00:00:11,787 --> 00:00:17,400
The 2010s marked a paradigm shift as private companies achieved orbital capability.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:27,750
SpaceX's Falcon 9, first launched in 2010, became the first orbital-class rocket with a reusable first stage, landing vertically on autonomous drone ships.

5
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:38,087
Reusability reduced launch costs from approximately $10,000 per kilogram on the Space Shuttle to roughly $2,600 per kilogram on Falcon 9.

6
00:00:38,087 --> 00:00:56,175
SpaceX's Starship, standing 120 meters tall and generating 74 meganewtons of thrust, is the largest rocket ever built; its fully reusable design targets a cost below $100 per kilogram to low Earth orbit and is intended to transport up to 100 passengers per trip to Mars.

7
00:00:56,175 --> 00:01:07,487
NASA's Artemis program, launched with Artemis I in 2022, aims to establish a permanent lunar outpost near the south pole, where water ice exists in permanently shadowed craters.

8
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:12,425
The Moon is envisioned as a proving ground for the technologies needed for Mars missions.

9
00:01:12,425 --> 00:01:33,225
Significant biomedical barriers remain: cosmic radiation exposes astronauts to doses equivalent to multiple CT scans per year; microgravity causes bone mineral density loss of approximately 1% per month and muscle atrophy; and the psychological effects of isolation in deep space for missions lasting 18 to 36 months are not fully understood.

10
00:01:33,225 --> 00:01:45,337
Nuclear thermal propulsion, using a nuclear reactor to heat propellant, could reduce transit time to Mars from eight months to roughly three months, decreasing radiation exposure and supply requirements.

